---
import ContentPageLayout from '../layouts/ContentPageLayout.astro';
import FeatureCard from '../components/FeatureCard.astro';
import CardDecor from '../components/CardDecor.astro';
import { Code2, WifiOff, GitFork } from 'lucide-preact';

const title = 'Open Source PDF Editor - Free, MIT Licensed | PDkef';
const description =
  'A free, open source PDF editor that runs entirely in your browser. Includes a one-minute test you can run yourself to confirm nothing is uploaded. MIT licensed.';

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'What license is PDkef released under?',
    answer:
      "The MIT license, for the code we wrote. It's about as permissive as open source licenses get: read it, fork it, modify it, sell something built on it. Every runtime dependency is MIT or Apache 2.0 too, which is a deliberate constraint rather than a coincidence.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I verify that my files really are not uploaded?',
    answer:
      "The fastest way takes about a minute and doesn't involve reading any code: open your browser's developer tools, switch the Network tab to Offline, then load a PDF, sign it, and download it. It all still works. A tool that sent your file to a server could not do that.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I self-host it or run my own copy?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Clone the repository, run npm install and npm run build, and serve the dist folder from anything that can serve static files. There is no backend to stand up, no database, and no environment variables to configure.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I contribute or report a bug?',
    answer: 'Yes, contributions and bug reports are both welcome on GitHub. Issues and discussions are open.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the free version limited, with a paid version being the "real" editor?',
    answer:
      "No. There is one version and it's the whole thing. Nothing here is a demo, a trial, or a reduced build of something bigger sold elsewhere.",
  },
];
---

<ContentPageLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  breadcrumb="Open source"
  icon={Code2}
  kicker="MIT licensed"
  h1="A Free, Open Source PDF Editor"
  subhead="Every tool here runs in your browser, and the code behind it is public. That matters more than it sounds: it means the claim that your files never leave your device is something you can check for yourself rather than something you have to believe."
  primaryCta={{ href: '/edit-pdf/', label: 'Try the PDF editor' }}
  faq={faq}
>
  <FeatureCard density="flow" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
    <CardDecor sketch="arcs" icon={Code2} iconPos="tr" iconSize={280} />
    <span class="mb-2 inline-block text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)]">Why it's open</span>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-0 text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-text)]">"We never see your files" is easy to say</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 mt-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      Every online PDF tool says something reassuring on its homepage. Most of them upload your document to a server,
      process it there, and delete it afterwards, which is a perfectly normal way to build software. The awkward part
      is that from the outside, a site that deletes your file an hour later and a site that doesn't look identical.
      You get a promise and a privacy policy, and no way to tell the difference.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      PDkef is architecturally incapable of the thing it promises not to do: there is no server to upload to. The <a class="text-[var(--color-primary)] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[var(--color-primary-hover)]" href="https://github.com/shlomsh/pdkef" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">source is on GitHub</a>
      under the MIT license so you can confirm that, but you don't have to read a line of it. The next card is a faster test.
    </p>
  </FeatureCard>

  <FeatureCard density="flow" variant="highlight" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
    <CardDecor sketch="rings" icon={WifiOff} iconPos="br" iconSize={280} />
    <span class="mb-2 inline-block text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)]">One minute, no code</span>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-0 text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-text)]">Prove it to yourself: use it with the network switched off</h2>
    <p class="mb-6 mt-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      This is the test worth knowing, because it works on any web tool, not just this one. Software that secretly
      uploads your file cannot function without a connection. Take the connection away and watch what happens.
    </p>
    <ol class="m-0 mb-6 flex list-none flex-col gap-5 p-0">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="mt-[0.1rem] flex size-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-soft)] text-[0.8rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-primary)]">1</span>
        <span class="text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--color-text)]"><strong class="font-semibold">Open any tool here</strong> and let the page finish loading.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="mt-[0.1rem] flex size-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-soft)] text-[0.8rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-primary)]">2</span>
        <span class="text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--color-text)]"><strong class="font-semibold">Turn off your wifi,</strong> or open developer tools and set the Network tab to Offline. Then put the phone in airplane mode, if that's easier.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="mt-[0.1rem] flex size-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-soft)] text-[0.8rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-primary)]">3</span>
        <span class="text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--color-text)]"><strong class="font-semibold">Load a PDF, edit it, and download the result.</strong> Merge some files, sign a form, delete a paragraph. All of it still works.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mb-4 mt-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      Nothing about that is a trick of caching. The PDF is read, edited and written by your own browser, using your
      device's processor, and the finished file is handed back to you locally. There is no round trip to interrupt,
      which is also why you can <a class="text-[var(--color-primary)] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[var(--color-primary-hover)]" href="/pdf-fill-and-sign-app/">install it and use it on a plane</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      That isn't left to good intentions either. The site ships a Content Security Policy with
      <strong class="font-semibold">connect-src 'self'</strong>,
      which instructs your browser to refuse any connection to another origin. If a future change ever tried to send
      your document somewhere, the browser would block it rather than trust us to have got it right.
    </p>
  </FeatureCard>

  <FeatureCard density="flow" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
    <CardDecor sketch="grid" icon={GitFork} iconPos="tr" iconSize={280} />
    <span class="mb-2 inline-block text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)]">Built on other people's work</span>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-0 text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-text)]">What's underneath, and how to run your own</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 mt-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      The heavy lifting comes from open source projects that were here long before this one. <strong class="font-semibold">pdf.js</strong>
      reads and renders PDFs, <strong class="font-semibold">pdf-lib</strong> writes them, <strong class="font-semibold">Astro</strong>
      and <strong class="font-semibold">Preact</strong> build the interface, and <strong class="font-semibold">signature_pad</strong>
      handles the drawing surface. Every one is MIT or Apache 2.0 licensed and makes no network calls of its own, which
      is a hard requirement here rather than a nice-to-have: a single dependency phoning home would undo the whole
      premise. The complete list with license texts is on the <a class="text-[var(--color-primary)] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[var(--color-primary-hover)]" href="/licenses/">open source licenses page</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      Running your own copy is genuinely simple, because there's nothing to run: clone the repository, build it, and
      serve the resulting folder from any static host, or straight off your own machine. No backend, no database, no
      keys to configure. If your workplace would rather this lived on an internal server than on someone else's
      domain, that's a completely reasonable thing to want, and the license says yes.
    </p>
  </FeatureCard>
</ContentPageLayout>
